    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Previous studies have demonstrated that sleep plays an important role in learning and memory, and that lack of sleep impairs memory consolidation. Starting with the observation that rodents performed poorly on hippocampal-dependent memory tests at certain times of the day, we sought to determine: 1) how the circadian system modulates learning, and 2) how sleep deprivation (SD) impairs learning and memory as a function of circadian phase. The present study evaluated the cognitive function of animals with intact circadian rhythms using variations of the Novel Object Recognition (NOR) test at four different time points of a 12-hour light/dark cycle – light onset (9:00am), mid-light (3:00pm), dark onset (9:00pm), and mid-dark (3:00am) – with or without SD. Although the tests on the effects of SD were inconclusive, animals tested with the simple NOR test demonstrated equivalent learning at all daily time points, while animals tested with the complex NOR test demonstrated greater performance at the mid-light and mid-dark time points. These results suggest that time of day effects modulate learning and memory, and that the degree to which time influences cognitive performance is dependent on the complexity of the cognitive task.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
